Divers, surfers, sailors, water skiers, cavers, and swimmers are often exposed to many elements in the water and need to wear protective clothing.
The diving suits are wet, semi-dry and dry. Semi-dry suits are an attempt to combine the advantages of dry and wet suits.
The main material used is rubber, cloth, neoprene. A great option for diving in ice water and hunting underwater.
When a person plans to dive or swim in very cold water, a diving suit dry.
It is made of neoprene and fabric that contains air to provide protective pockets of air between the body and the freezing water.
It may interest you:
Wet-type wetsuits are an excellent option for shallow diving. A feature of such products is the possibility of water penetration under the upper layers of the wetsuit.
For the manufacture of wet models, foam is used. It is not recommended to use such equipment for immersion in cold water.
They are supposed to be worn well to avoid excessive loss of body heat and are often customized to fit the diver's body.
They are elastic, easy to put on, reliably adjustable and snug to the diver's body, provide high-quality comfort and sealing. The increased water resistance of the material allows the diver to stay in the water as long as possible.
They can come in individual pieces or in two pieces and are generally used in water temperatures between 50 and 68 degrees Fahrenheit (10 to 20 degrees Celsius).
According to the water temperature, there are two main types of diving suits: soft diving suits, also known as ambient pressure suits; and hard diving suits, also known as atmospheric pressure suits.
Ambient pressure suits are designed to protect the user from cold water and provide defense against sharp objects, such as coral reefs.
This type of suit can come in several different forms, such as diving skins, wetsuits, dry suits, semi-dry suits, and hot water suits.
They are sometimes called "stinger suits" because they protect the wearer from jellyfish stings, abrasions, and sun exposure.
Atmospheric Pressure Suits Hot water diving suits and atmospheric pressure suits are generally used for extremely deep dives and in icy waters.
It is important to consider the purpose of the immersion, the operating conditions, the quality indicators and the thickness of the material among others.
