They are usually located at the rear of the wing, on the side of the fuselage next to the ailerons. The fins can be placed in different positions for certain situations.
When extended, they increase the sag and, in most cases, the chord and wing surface, resulting in a increased lift and drag and in a reduction in stall speed.
In addition to the function of generating more lift, the extended fins also provide more resistance to the air. This is useful, for example, for landing approaches, especially in the field of ultralight flights, which often have to be steep, but still have to be done at low speed.
But there is also a maximum speed, which is determined by the construction. Elevation gain is greatest in the early stages.
Extending the flaps also increases resistance, which, for the most part, is a good thing.
Many aircraft, especially large ones, use flaps for takeoff. Without the elevation gain that ailerons provide, most large aircraft simply would not be able to go fast enough, or have enough runway, to take off.
That's quite different from landing, when planes typically use 25-40 degrees of fins.
By extending the ailerons a bit, the aircraft benefits from the increased lift (due to bank), but does not pay the high form-drag penalty caused by fully extended ailerons.
This gives you two distinct advantages: 1) you have a slower stall speed, which means you can land slower, and 2) you produce more drag, allowing you to fly at a steeper angle of descent towards the runway.
